<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-eigen-values-vectors-zero-ident-scalar">
<title>Zero, identity, and scalar matrices</title>

<introduction><p>
	For each matrix:
	<ol>
		<li> Determine the characteristic polynomial in factored form and the eigenvalues. </li>
		<li> For each eigenvalue, determine a basis for the corresponding eigenspace. </li>
	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> The <m>n \times n</m> zero matrix, <m>\zerovec_n</m>. </p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>
		<li><p>
			Characteristic polynomial: <m>c_{\zerovec_n}(\lambda) = \lambda^n</m>.
			Eigenvalue: <m>\lambda = 0</m>.
		</p></li>
		<li><p>
			Since <m>\zerovec_n \uvec{x} = 0 \uvec{x}</m> is true for all <m>\uvec{x}</m> in <m>\R^n</m>,
			the eigenspace <m>E_0(\zerovec_n)</m> is all of <m>\R^n</m>.
			Therefore, the standard basis for <m>\R^n</m> is also a basis for <m>E_0(\zerovec_n)</m>.
		</p></li>
	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> The <m>n \times n</m> identity matrix, <m>I_n</m>. </p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>
		<li><p>
			Characteristic polynomial: <m>c_{I_n}(\lambda) = {(\lambda - 1)}^n</m>.
			Eigenvalue: <m>\lambda = 1</m>.
		</p></li>
		<li><p>
			Since <m>I_n \uvec{x} = 1 \uvec{x}</m> is true for all <m>\uvec{x}</m> in <m>\R^n</m>,
			the eigenspace <m>E_1(I_n)</m> is all of <m>\R^n</m>.
			Therefore, the standard basis for <m>\R^n</m> is also a basis for <m>E_1(I_n)</m>.
		</p></li>
	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> A scalar matrix <m>k I_n</m>, for a fixed scalar <m>k</m>. </p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>
		<li><p>
			Characteristic polynomial: <m>c_{(k I_n)}(\lambda) = {(\lambda - k)}^n</m>.
			Eigenvalue: <m>\lambda = k</m>.
		</p></li>
		<li><p>
			Since <m>(k I_n) \uvec{x} = k \uvec{x}</m> is true for all <m>\uvec{x}</m> in <m>\R^n</m>,
			the eigenspace <m>E_k(k I_n)</m> is all of <m>\R^n</m>.
			Therefore, the standard basis for <m>\R^n</m> is also a basis for <m>E_k(k I_n)</m>.
		</p></li>
	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
